This file describes bugs that are not yet fixed and features that are not
yet implemented. Rewritten 2026-10-17 after the v1.1.33 release from a
full read-through of lib/, cli/, daemon/, hub/, snmpd/, the Makefile, CI
and the man pages. Every item cites file:line as of 1.1.33 (ae2af46).
Confidence is "confirmed" (full code path read) unless marked
"plausible" (depends on an OS/library fact not verifiable in-repo).
Items from the previous TODO that are still open are carried over in
section 10; everything previously marked [FIXED] is gone from here and
lives in CHANGELOG.md. Cleaned again 2026-10-30 at 1.1.69: the [FIXED in
1.1.34 … 1.1.69] entries that had piled up since were removed (each is in
its CHANGELOG.md section), and what they still left open is listed below
as its own item.

Priority legend: HIGH = wrong or dangerous behaviour reachable in a
normal deployment; MED = incorrect result, data/resource leak or a
documented feature that does not work; LOW = hygiene, edge cases, dead
code, doc drift.

The bhyve/VM track is in TODO2.

=== 1. Blocker: the rootless (1.0.0) model is not actually reachable ===

* (HIGH) exec_in_jail follow-ups:
    - no on-hardware run yet: the whole rootless `crate run` path
      (ensure_user_root → create_jail → mounts → exec_in_jail …) has
      never been exercised end to end; docs/on-hardware-validation.md
      §4.1 is the first thing to run.
    - NAT mode under rootless (acquire_fw_lease / release_fw_lease,
      1.1.57) and rootless bridge mode on a host with forwarding=0 are
      not validated on hardware either (on-hardware-validation §4).

* (HIGH) remaining direct privileged calls under rootless:
    - Util::ensureKernelModuleIsLoaded (ipfw_nat, if_bridge, ng_*)
      now checks kldfind first and, unprivileged, tells the operator
      which module to load instead of failing in kldload — loading
      modules stays a host-admin step by design.
    - lib/inter_dns.cpp writes /etc/hosts and the unbound conf.d file
      directly (root-only by nature). 1.1.56: refuses up front as
      non-root with the reason; moving the rebuild into crated as a
      verb is still open.
    - stack.cpp (bridge create, unbound on :53, /etc/hosts injection
      into jails) — stack is root-only today. 1.1.56: `stack up`/`down`
      refuse up front as non-root with the reason instead of failing
      half-way with containers already started.
    - `logs` is overridable from ~/.config/crate/crate.yml, so a user
      still chooses where their own audit trail goes (the daemon's
      audit.log is the record that counts for privops).
    - `create_bridge` only accepts cloner names (bridge<N>); a spec
      with auto_create_bridge and a renamed bridge (e.g. "lan0") gets a
      clear 400 under rootless — needs create + `name` rename in the
      daemon if that is wanted.
* (HIGH, bug hunt 1.1.71) privops is not a boundary towards the host
  (docs said otherwise; trust-model.md Plane 1 and CLAUDE.md now say
  so). A member of the privops group, or an admin token, is root on the
  host through gated verbs: mount_nullfs from any host path outside the
  tenant root (sourceForeign, privops_authz_pure.cpp:147-160 — /etc
  read-write, then exec_in_jail as root, then a line in /etc/crontab);
  apply_devfs_rules, whose rules are checked for shape only
  (privops_pure.cpp:790-813 — `path * unhide` puts the host's disks, mem
  and kmem into the own jail); and the host-wide ipfw verbs (§2). The
  cross-tenant gates of 1.1.12-1.1.71 therefore stop accidents between
  trusted operators, not a hostile tenant. To make rootless multi-tenant
  real: a source allow list (the caller's home, its own slice, the GUI
  runtime sockets), devfs rules built from device classes (DevicesPure)
  instead of free globs, and the ipfw verbs tied to the caller's fw
  lease slot.
* (HIGH, bug hunt 1.1.71, plausible: EPERM paths not run on hardware)
  rootless runs reach operations that have no privops route:
  Mount("devfs") (mount.cpp:33, run.cpp) and the unionfs of `cow:`
  (run.cpp) call nmount(2) directly — EPERM for a non-root caller. (The
  scripts and the crontab chmod went through chroot(8) too; since 1.1.75
  they run inside the jail.)
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.75) scripts/run:begin, run:before-create-jail
  and run:after-remove-jail run in a throw-away jail(8) (ScriptsPure::
  hookPlace), which needs root: a rootless run refuses a spec that has
  them. A privops verb that creates such a jail on the caller's own tree
  and runs one command in it would lift that.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) crate.yml's securelevel / children_max defaults
  are lost on the privops path: run.cpp:1200-1204 computes the effective
  values, but run_jail.cpp:75-78,108-111 sends only spec.*, and the
  post-create `jail -m` is skipped when privops is active
  (run.cpp:1304,1313) — root runs too once the socket exists.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) rootless NAT counts bridge jails as users of
  the shared NAT rule (fw_lease_pure.cpp:11-18; run.cpp:1240,1266,2088):
  with a bridge jail already running, a NAT jail never sets up `nat
  50000` (no outbound), and when the bridge jail is the last to go the
  shared rule stays.
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.72) a tenant's own `crate run` teardown sends
  destroy_jail with the jid as the name (run_jail.cpp:245), which 1.1.72
  gates strictly by jid. A jid the registry lost (a crated restart
  before 1.1.72, when /var/db/crate did not exist and nothing persisted)
  is now DenyUnownedJid, the fallback jail_remove is EPERM for a
  non-root caller, and the jail stays. Send the kernel name (the xname)
  instead of the jid.
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.73) root's state files stay directly in
  /var/run/crate, which is searchable (0711) since 1.1.73:
  spec-registry.txt, network-leases*.txt, ctx-*, pfctl.lock and the
  stacks' dns-<network> directories are readable by any local user
  wherever they are 0644 — container names, IPs, .crate paths (mostly
  what jls shows anyway). Move them into /var/run/crate/0 (with a
  one-time move of the old files) or create them 0600.
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.73) the HTTP export (routes.cpp, ARTIFACT_DIR,
  now /var/run/crate/0, 0700) still goes through execPipeline's
  shared-output mode: an existing file of the same name is truncated,
  and the archive is 0644 (unreachable for others behind the 0700
  directory). execPipelineToPrivateFile + rename would match the CLI
  export.

=== 2. crated privops / HTTP — security ===

* (MED, residual of the 1.1.35 canonicalisation) the realpath() check
  and the nmount()/jail -c/devfs call are two steps; a symlink swapped
  in between still wins the race. Closing it needs fd-based operation:
  open(O_RESOLVE_BENEATH) relative to the caller's prefix and
  nmount with "fspath" replaced by an fd ("fd" iov) / jail path via
  fdescfs, or at least a second realpath() after the operation with
  rollback. Same for lib/run_gui.cpp's mount targets (lstat then
  mount). Low practical exposure (needs a compromised jail racing
  its own operator's `crate run`), but the only remaining gap in the
  path chain.

* (by design, documented) a pool-scoped ADMIN token can POST
  /imports/:name + /containers/:name/start for a name in its pool and
  have crated run that .crate as root (the spec may declare host
  shares/scripts). trust-model §2b already states admin tokens are
  host-wide, not pool-confined; only viewer/pool-scoped tokens carry
  isolation. Making this real needs the spawned `crate run` to drop to
  the token's mapped uid — part of the rootless §1 work, not a gate.

* (MED) Synchronous HTTP handlers remain synchronous — by choice in
  1.1.37 (the worker pool now absorbs them): routes.cpp stop (≤ 11 s),
  restart (stop phase ≤ 11 s, then spawn), export (`tar | xz --extreme`
  inline, minutes). `crate migrate` reads the export response (file,
  size, sha256) and then downloads, so making export asynchronous
  (202 + poll) needs a migrate client change in the same release;
  stop/restart could return 202 after SIGTERM with a detached
  wait/KILL child, but that changes the documented `stopped:true`
  contract. Decide API shape first, then do both ends.

* (LOW, left open by 1.1.70) the devfs ruleset claims
  (privops_handlers.cpp g_devfsClaims) live in crated's memory: after a
  daemon restart every set left from before counts as taken while it
  holds rules (nobody deletes a set at teardown — tenants have no verb
  for it, the root path does not), until reboot. crate run steps to the
  next number, 32 times at most.
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.70) root-path runs take 10000 + pid % 50000
  too, unchecked: two live root runs whose pids differ by 50000 share a
  set, and the second one's `delset` empties the first one's (kern.pid_max
  is 99999). A per-run number handed out by crated, or a check that the
  set is empty, would close it.
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.70) libifconfig's own sockets (ifconfig_open)
  have no close-on-exec. The exec paths into jails close everything
  above stderr now, but host-side children — Xephyr / x11vnc /
  websockify (run_gui.cpp), socat (run_services.cpp) — still inherit
  them.
* (LOW) Registry write happens after `jail -c` returns (:663-671);
  until then the new jid/path are unowned (bootstrap Allow).
* (LOW) privops_socket_mode gets no tightness warning unlike
  listen.unix_mode.
* (LOW) handlers at privops_handlers.cpp :171,560,577 return raw
  strerror/e.what() that may echo host paths. (jsonError escaping
  fixed in 1.1.35.)
* (LOW, bug hunt 1.1.70) JidOwnerRegistry::findOwnerByPath
  (jid_owner_registry.cpp:120-129) answers from stale rows — byJid /
  byName drop dead entries (dropIfStaleLocked_, :93-118), byPath does
  not, contrary to daemon/main.cpp:150-156 and trust-model.md:149-151.
  Practically masked (paths carry a random suffix under the owner's own
  slice), but the lookup should skip dead matches.
* (LOW, bug hunt 1.1.70, hardening) the ipfw verbs stay host-wide:
  delete_ipfw_nat / remove_ipfw_rule (privops_handlers.cpp:393-403,
  776-787) fall to authorize()'s default Allow like add_ipfw_rule /
  configure_ipfw_nat (by design, trust-model.md:154-157, which does not
  name the delete verbs). Since 1.1.57 the fw lease gives a per-jail
  slot — gating rule / NAT numbers by the caller's leased slot would
  make all four tenant-scoped.
* (MED, left open by 1.1.74) the privops request deadline covers the
  header and the data, not the descriptor part: a request whose header
  announces descriptors and that never sends the SCM_RIGHTS message
  still waits in nvlist_recv (libnv's fd receive select()s without a
  timeout). The per-uid cap (16, privops_listener.cpp) keeps one uid to a
  quarter of the 64 slots; four uids of one tenant, or colluding
  tenants, still take them all. Fix: receive the descriptors ourselves
  with a deadline, or a libnv receive with a timeout.
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.74) the HTTP stream cap (8 log-follow + SSE,
  routes.cpp) is per daemon, not per token: one viewer token can hold
  all 8 and other viewers' streams get 503 (the rest of the API keeps
  its workers). A per-token share of the cap would close it.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) devfs ruleset claims can be exhausted: the
  claim is recorded before the request is validated
  (privops_handlers.cpp:1134-1144, 1689-1696) and released only when the
  claimed mount is gone. A tenant with one live jail claims 10000..59999
  (an empty `rules` is answered 400 but claimed); another tenant's
  `crate run` then gets 32 x 403, and run.cpp:1041-1076 only warns and
  goes on — its jail gets devfs with NO ruleset, the host's whole /dev.
  A root call with force=true (:1697-1701) overwrites a live tenant
  claim and delsets that set. Fix: claim after the handler succeeded,
  one claim per mount, no force over another uid's live claim,
  exhaustion fatal in crate run.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) cross-pool restart through the container name
  taken from the jail PATH: control_socket.cpp:375,408 and
  routes.cpp:679 gate the pool by kernel name but pick the .crate by
  RunPure::containerNameOfJail, which falls back to the jail directory
  name. A privops tenant creates `name=alice-x
  path=/var/run/crate/<uid>/jail-bob-db-deadbeef`; alice's pool socket
  restarts `alice-x`, and bob's .crate runs as root, recorded for alice.
  Needs a root registry, i.e. the §1 ensure_user_root fix. Fix:
  pool-gate the container name too.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) GET /api/v1/containers is not filtered by the
  token's pool (routes.cpp:178-186, 1163-1174): a viewer token of pool
  `alice` sees every jail's name, path, IP and hostname. The
  control-socket list filters (control_socket.cpp:159-177).
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) /metrics needs no token and runs 2 jls plus one
  rctl per jail per request, at up to 100 rps per IP
  (routes.cpp:1201-1208, metrics.cpp:169-259), and lists jail names and
  the VNC / WS ports 1.1.36 hid elsewhere. Fix: a few seconds of cache,
  an optional token.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71, plausible) control sockets check only the
  peer's egid from getpeereid (control_socket_pure.cpp:256-257): a
  member through a supplementary group (`pw groupmod … -m alice`, the
  usual way) gets 403 although the kernel let them connect. Fix:
  getgrouplist(uid).
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) ws-console: pre-auth slowloris — SO_RCVTIMEO is
  per recv and the handshake reads one byte at a time, so a byte every
  14 s holds a slot forever and 64 connections lock the console out
  (ws_console.cpp:72-84, 339-347; httplib on :9800 likely the same,
  plausible). The console also speaks cleartext while crated.8's example
  binds "::": the admin token that opens a root shell crosses the
  network in clear. Fix: a handshake deadline; TLS or loopback only.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) control-socket PATCH puts its values into the
  rctl rule unvalidated (control_socket.cpp:424-428); the privops path
  uses RetunePure::validateRctlValue.
* (LOW, bug hunt 1.1.71) JidOwnerRegistry::lookupByName
  (jid_owner_registry.cpp:105-118) takes the first match: a stale first
  row is dropped and "unknown" returned instead of the live row of the
  same name (variant of the byPath item above).
  SpecRegistry::effectivePath (spec_registry.cpp:33-46) is a non-atomic
  lazy static — the first concurrent start / restart race on the
  std::string. `require_client_cert` is parsed and never used
  (config.cpp:63-64, server.cpp:175-178; auth.h:115 promises an mTLS CN
  check). WsConsole::stop relies on shutdown() waking accept()
  (ws_console.cpp:422-429; plausible on older FreeBSD).

=== 3. Networking runtime ===

* (MED, left open by 1.1.38) IPv4 forwarding is restored only when
  allowOutbound(): run.cpp enables net.inet.ip.forwarding for every jail
  with `net:` when it was 0 (root path), run_net.cpp restores it only in
  the outbound teardown, so inbound-only jails leave it at 1. Rootless
  goes through release_fw_lease (1.1.57). Checked at 1.1.69.
* (LOW) ipfw_ops.cpp:85 treats EINVAL from IP_FW_XGET as "native API
  available" — EINVAL can also mean an unsupported opcode on an older
  kernel; needs a FreeBSD check before changing.
* (LOW) mac_ops.cpp removeUgidfwRules deletes by list-line index (a
  concurrent add shifts indexes); the native path should match rules
  by content.
* (LOW) healthcheck `retries: 0` means the first failure is UNHEALTHY —
  that is the documented meaning of zero retries; left as is.
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.74) the `firewall:` policy and bridge-mode
  SNAT/rdr still share the anchor crate/<jail>: the policy load carries
  the translation text along (run.cpp, bridgePfTranslation), so either
  one cannot be reloaded alone. Sub-anchors (crate/<jail>/nat,
  crate/<jail>/policy) would separate them; the teardown flushes would
  have to recurse. Also decide whether `default: block` should drop new
  connections TO the jail that no rdr covers — since 1.1.74 the policy
  covers what the jail sends only (`from <jail ip>`), inbound is the
  host ruleset's.
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.74) netgraph mode and the X11 socket isolation
  (a read-only nullfs of /tmp/.X11-unix/.crate-*, a hard link to the
  server's socket) are not run by CI: on-hardware-validation.md §4p.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) NAT `ipv6: true` always fails:
  RunPure::epairIp6 builds "fd00:cra7:e::…" (run_pure.cpp:169) — 'r' is
  not a hex digit — and the address is refused (run.cpp:2055). The epair
  teardown is armed only at run.cpp:2071, so every attempt, and every
  restart-policy retry, leaves an epair pair. Variant of the §10 extras
  item.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71, plausible) bridge + pool with ipfw:
  auto_fw_pure.cpp:197-205 installs only `nat N … out via <ext>`, no
  inbound `nat N`, so replies and redir_port are not translated back;
  the hairpin `tcp from me to me` rule (:235-237) sends all of the
  host's loopback TCP through the first jail's NAT.
* (LOW, bug hunt 1.1.71) passthrough with static_mac (run.cpp:1909) does
  not restore the NIC's MAC when the interface comes back: the host NIC
  keeps 58:9c:fc:…. NAT DNS takes the host's first nameserver
  (net.cpp:163); with local_unbound that is 127.0.0.1 and the jail has
  no DNS (plausible).

=== 4. crate stack ===

* (MED) allocateIpPool is in-process only — not persisted, not
  reconciled with network_lease; two `stack up` runs of different
  stacks on one subnet can hand out the same address. Needs the lease
  file (lib/ip_alloc) or a stack-level lease under /var/run/crate.
* (LOW) no unit test round-trips every Spec field through mergeSpecs:
  lib/spec.cpp is not in TEST_LINK_SRCS (yaml-cpp), so the test needs
  either a pure "field list" or a FreeBSD functional test.
* (LOW) network_policy: a rule whose `proto` is omitted produces an
  ipfw syntax error at addRule (now cleaned up, but still fails the
  stack); validate rule.proto at parse.
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.70) a stack never puts a member ON its bridge:
  only a spec that already says `options/net: {mode: bridge, bridge:
  <stack bridge>}` gets a pool address (StackPure::poolEligible); a
  NAT member or a `network: auto` one without `bridge:` is warned about
  and left out of the stack's hosts/DNS. Injecting mode + bridge for
  such members (a `containers/<name>/network: <net>` key?) is a design
  decision, not a fix.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) StackSpec::netInfo substitutes only when vars
  are given (stack_spec.cpp:66): a member without vars and without --var
  keeps `ip: "${DB_IP:-10.0.0.5}/24"` or `bridge: "${BR:-bridge0}"`
  literal — the address is not reserved in the pool (stack.cpp:650;
  another member can get it), unbound gets garbage local-data
  (:218-223), hostsCommand throws after bridge and DNS are up
  (:320-323), and a default-bridge member gets no pool address
  (:1103-1104). CHANGELOG 1.1.71 promises the substitution without
  --var.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) stack members are found by kernel name only
  (stack.cpp:837,1218,1298): on a root host without crated the jail is
  named by its jid, so `stack status` shows everything stopped, `down`
  stops nothing and still prints "Stack stopped." with exit 0, `exec`
  says not running. Names are global: `stack down a.yml` also stops
  other stacks' or standalone `db_pid*`.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) `vars/<K>/from_file` secrets go into the argv
  of the detached `crate run --var K=V` (stack.cpp:96), which lives as
  long as the container: every local user sees them in ps.
  crate.5:1420-1428 promises only that the secret stays out of the
  .crate. `crate restart` of a member loses the vars.
* (LOW, bug hunt 1.1.71) the dns-<network> directories are shared by
  stacks with the same network name (stack.cpp:271-307): `down` of one
  stack kills the other's unbound.

=== 5. Spec parsing, config, registries ===

* (LOW) firewall/allow-tcp|udp with a map value and mac_portacl
  non-map / allow_ports non-sequence are still silently ignored
  (spec.cpp firewall / security_advanced blocks) — mirror the
  files/ handling.
* (LOW) Duplicate top-level keys: only `scripts` is checked
  (spec.cpp); other sections are processed twice (lists append) or
  last-wins. (plausible — yaml-cpp iteration semantics)
* (LOW) locs.cpp:18-22 static initialiser calls getSysctlString; a
  throw there is std::terminate before main.
* (LOW) `unless-stopped` == `always` in cli/main.cpp; no backoff
  between restarts.
* (LOW, left open by 1.1.71) the `.local` key list (profile_pure.cpp
  forbiddenLocalKeys) is a deny list two levels deep: `run/user: root`,
  `firewall` (a default `allow`) and `options` details (`tor: {…}`,
  `net:` modes) still loosen what the profile chose, and a new
  security/ or ipc/ key passes until someone adds it. An allow list of
  what a .local may set (pkg, options names, limits, run/args, shares
  under $HOME) would close the class.
  The bug hunt after 1.1.71 found more of the class: `security:
  {secure-level: -1}` passes (normaliseKey gives secure_level, the
  parser also takes secure-level, spec.cpp:1011); a key written
  `${X:-scripts}:` is checked before substitution and becomes `scripts:`
  in the build (profile.cpp:199-209 vs spec.cpp:1552);
  terminal/devfs_ruleset, x11 and clipboard are not listed.
* (LOW, bug hunt 1.1.70) `devices:` merges by shape: Profile::mergeYaml
  takes the overlay when the shapes differ (profile.cpp:148), so a
  `.local` with `devices: [gpu]` over a profile's `{sound: true}`
  silently drops sound (the parser takes both forms,
  spec.cpp:1293-1316). Errs towards less access. Merge by class, as
  `options` is (profile.cpp:52-111).
* (LOW, bug hunt 1.1.70) privacy/hostname "web.example.org." passes
  NetAddrPure::isHostname (netaddr_pure.cpp:89-103, via
  privacy_pure.cpp:115) and `crate validate`, but the privops
  create_jail validator refuses the empty label (privops_pure.cpp:210,
  :544): rootless runs fail at jail creation, root ones accept it.
  Validate with PrivOpsPure::validateHostname in parseHostname.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) `pkg/keep-pkg: false` is ignored: the
  hand-written Spec copy constructor (spec.cpp:42-77) does not copy
  pkgKeepPkg — the only top-level member it misses — and createCrate
  gets spec.preprocess(), a copy (spec.cpp:208; main.cpp:176,
  profile.cpp:339, stack.cpp:1152). The image always keeps pkg and
  /var/db/pkg. Fix: add it; better `= default` with clone wrappers for
  the unique_ptr members.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) `limits`: crate.5:650-655 and SpecPure
  (spec_pure.cpp:410-416) accept 25 RCTL keys, privops
  (retune_pure.cpp:14-38,74-84) 15. cputime, wallclock, coredumpsize,
  pseudoterminals, nsem*, nshm and msgq* pass `crate validate`, get 400
  under privops, and the run dies (run_jail.cpp:297-305). One shared key
  list and value format.
* (MED, bug hunt 1.1.71) validate() runs on the raw spec before
  preprocess() (main.cpp:173-175, profile.cpp:334-335, stack.cpp:1140):
  crate.5:1312-1321's own `network: external` + `ip:` + `extra:` example
  is refused (still mode=nat raw, spec_pure.cpp:362-369, 384-385), and a
  `mode: bridge` that relies on crate.yml's default_bridge gets
  "requires 'bridge' field".
* (LOW, bug hunt 1.1.71) script order: the array-of-arrays form names
  its entries script#N in a std::map (spec.cpp:167-170, scripts.cpp:24),
  so script#10 runs before script#2; the map form runs alphabetically,
  not in document order.
* (LOW, bug hunt 1.1.71) `network: auto` depends on key order: a later
  `options: [net]`, `options: {net: …}` or an options list with tor
  silently resets the mode to NAT (spec.cpp:616-632 vs 637-643, 666,
  852), and the conflict check is order-dependent too. Apply it after
  the loop.
* (LOW, bug hunt 1.1.71, plausible) scalar sections are silently
  ignored: `pkg: chromium`, `base: host-ro`, `run: …`, `dirs: …`, `zfs:
  …` have no IsMap check (spec.cpp:421,448,475,502,907). Variant of the
  firewall / mac_portacl item above.
* (LOW, bug hunt 1.1.71) unvalidated strings reach host config:
  gui/resolution and gui/gpu_device go into the host Xorg's xorg.conf
  (run_gui_pure.cpp:72,84 — quotes and newlines inject sections),
  services/managed/rcvar into rc.conf. Trusted on the root path by
  design, but they should be validated.
* (LOW, found checking 1.1.73) crate.yml's `prefix` is parsed
  (config.cpp:24,72) and used nowhere — crate.5 calls it the "jail
  directory root" (default /var/run/crate), but the jail directories are
  fixed: /var/run/crate/0 for root, /var/run/crate/<uid> per user. Drop
  the key or make it the runtime root.

=== 6. crate doctor ===

* (LOW) doctor's auto-fw anchor probe (checkAutoFwRules) does not know
  the running jail's `_pid<N>` xname and probes `crate/<name>_pid?`
  literally — make it list `pfctl -a crate -s Anchors` and match the
  prefix.
* (LOW) `crate doctor --json` has no schema version field; consumers
  cannot tell 1.1.x output shapes apart.

=== 7. Lifecycle, storage, GUI, monitoring, DNS, VPN ===

* (LOW, left open by 1.1.70) the HTTP snapshot routes (routes.cpp
